th.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-26T16:46:11-07:00","id":1121089,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1120963-book-of-secrets-on-dtrpg?p=1121084#post1121084\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I guess it's because there's no index.<br /> <br /> A nice compromise might be to have it in the PDF info so you could use the ToC for a section or the PDF reader to get to a specific Merit.</div> </div> </div> </div>It does. At least it does on my phone.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/987-tuch","name":"Tuch","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar987_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-26T17:25:50-07:00","id":1121102,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>BurritoMage</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1120963-book-of-secrets-on-dtrpg?p=1121082#post1121082\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Just wait for the dissertation on trigger warnings in Book of the Fallen.</div> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"color:red\">Making fun of trigger warnings is disrespectful towards people with post-traumatic stress and against rule 1. Don't do it again.</span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-27T03:04:31-07:00","id":1121240,"title":"","content":"I've had the book since the errata, but I figure this is the best thread for discussing it.<br /> <br /> Personally, I like the book a lot.<br /> Definitely more than HDYDT; Which still, in my mind, is more a collection of house-rules than any solid objective guide based on the core Magick rules. Certainly good for inspiration and cherry-picking of things one might like, but best really treated as such.<br /> <br /> Now, the Book Of Secrets on the other hand has a much more <i>'Writing that didn't fit in the core book'</i> feel to it, and I especially welcome the huge collection of <b>Merits and Flaws</b> in a singular book. It really is hands-down one of the simplest, yet most convenient things so far.<br /> <br /> The <b>Secondary Abilities</b> I actually like a lot, though I *might* be in the minority there. I am not sure. I simply dislike the system of 'Hobby Talents' and 'Expert Knowledge' and the like that the 20th Editions introduced - the system of Secondary Talents that Mage 20th uses is simple and to the point: If you want your character to have an ability that is not present on the sheet, simply add it as an according skill, talent or knowledge and purchase as normal. Done. Simple. Neat.<br /> What I do *not* like about the section, or rather am confused about, is how some of the secondary abilities pretty much fall under already existing ones; Take climbing for example. Climbing rolls get covered by Athletics already, and if a character is quite proficient at it, can be purchased as a specialty. What advantage would a Climbing Secondary Ability give?<br /> The only way I can see where that would make sense is, if a secondary ability actually gives you the advantages of a specialization from the first dot already It's a much more narrow field than the general abilites of the standard character sheet - but in turn, you also get to be better at them. I've pondered a similiar way of allowing the Craftsman rule from M20 core to be spread to more than the suggested abilities of that section, but Secondary Abilities would make it cleaner.<br /> <br /> The <b>expanded combat systems</b> are okay. I like some of the ideas - especially how the book actually gives suggestions on how Magick could improve or enhance the individual combat maneuvers and tricks - though some of the maneuvers are *clearly* aimed at mook-level enemies only, or more cinematic-style adventures (As the mentioning of the old Sorceror's Crusade Swashbuckler's Handbook suggests in the same chapter) be it because of downright flat AoE level-damage or being able to mentally stun the onlookers through sheer flamboyancy. Which is fine, really, some people like a more cineastic mood and action-driven moment. Though I don't do much Mooks in my STing and prefer a more realistic approach.<br /> Which is one of the reasons why I prefer the <i>'Advanced Weapon Techiques'</i> section way more over the <i>'Stunts'</i> section.<br /> What I *love* though is the <i>'What Have I done'</i> section - dealing with the mental repercussions of a person with a relative normal upbringing (Which just happen to have become a Mage) having suddenly been forced to hurt or downright kill somebody. I might be biased in that regard though - I've argued the very same thing on these forums before, where people seemed to forget that at least young Mages don't possess the same kind of inhumanity that Vampires or Werewolves do, and do not tend to be badass, emotionless fighters or killers.<br /> <br /> <b>Cert\u00e1men</b> rules...eh. I never was really interested in Cert\u00e1men to be honest. Don't misunderstand me there, I like the fact that they are now back in a 20th Anniversary fashion, but I can't say if anything got improved, streamlined or changed because I mostly skipped that section.<br /> <br /> The section on making <b>Child Mages</b>, and the effects of old age - neat to have.<br /> <br /> When I saw the big section on <b>Computer Systems</b>, I clenched up a little because I was instinctively expecting the worst that Hollywood has to offer, but it is mostly quite sane, and well researched. I was surprised to find Van Eck attacks even explained. The actual game mechanics sverve into the Hollywoodesque a little, but that's fine I think - it's WoD, and we have the Digital Web, and Cyborgs and AIs.<br /> <br /> The big section on <b>Resonance and Synergy</b>, I admit, I'll have to re-read again a couple of times - it's not kept simple, and kind of repetitive in several statements. I still have to come up with a clear, simple image of it all in my head, though I liked the section on the Effects of Resonance, resonance cloaking, and how Prime Magick actually is a big catalyst to resonance energies in an area.<br /> <br /> The <b>Wonders</b> section gave me mixed feelings. I had my hopes up for a more simple, elegant Wonder creation process based on the one mentioned in the M20 core; But it seems, for better or for worse, that a good chunk of inspiration was drawn from Forged By Dragon's Fire - not necessarily a bad thing, given that a lot of people are used to it, but I still think an opportunity for streamlining it all was missed. That said, the section covers it all - all types of wonders, slight variations of the rules for technocratic devices, and a neat method of investing flaws and advantages into a wonder.<br /> The only thing that again made my eyelid twitch was that, once more, the example wonders had messed up types - Bullets specified as 'Gadgets' instead of 'Charms' and such. Oh well.<br /> <br /> The chapter on <b>Justice and Influence</b> was an okay read to me. Admittedly, just like Cert\u00e1men, I am not highly interested in the unified formalities among the organized Council of the Nine Tradtions - mostly because I think those things really, really break down quickly as you close in on individual Tradition-, Chantry or Cabal politics and rules. I can see how they are all enforced in a place like the defunct Horizon realm of the same name, but on the 'front-lines' as the Technocracy would call it..things become much, much more individual where the Tradition's side of things is concerned.<br /> I mean, 'lazyness' and 'Cowardice' are defined as Low Crimes in the Tradition writeup. Really now. Those things *scream* of 'Thought up in council chambers, laughed at at the Cabal round-table'. Well, except if Hermetics are involved. Bwahah.<br /> The Technocracy writeup for the same is spot-on on the other hand. Not much new from the Guide To The Technocracy, but like Cert\u00e1men now in a 20th book.<br /> The Disparate section..well, it's like the Tradition section, but enforcement of those rules throughout is much more believable - especially given that the Desparats are highly dependant on discipline, if they are going to keep up their game of hiding their nature from the other factions.<br /> <br /> Finally the <b>FAQ</b>..the section that is, for sure, going to continue on giving us reason for snarky comments and arguments on these forums. I don't mind its inclusion.<br /> Some parts of it are not very interesting to me - mostly Phil's explanations of his history with the game, or personal opinions on certain matters. <i>'Nice to know'</i> things at times that explain why he did some things the way he did them, but not important from a pure gaming perspective.<br /> But in other parts it answers some long standing mechanics questions in short, to the point words. And, as I explained in another thread, I highly prefer having an FAQ in an actual book instead of online. The reason is simple; Try to find the old White Wolf WoD FAQs that made up contents of mailing lists and websites years and years ago. They are hard to come by, and when a question comes up at the game table, I want to just reach out and have it in a single book, in easy reach. We try to avoid any online gadgets at our table.<br /> You can argue about it wasting word count, or not being intersted in Phil's opinions or the FAQ as a whole, but there are two more tomes coming up; I don't think anything got lost due to it taking up word count, and even if you don't like the FAQ, others might enjoy reading it. Let them.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Annnd that wraps up my review I guess.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":6606,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","user":"Ambrosia","date":". 07-27-2017, 06:13 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-27T06:54:02-07:00","id":1121285,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ambrosia</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1120963-book-of-secrets-on-dtrpg?p=1121240#post1121240\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The <b>Wonders</b> section gave me mixed feelings. I had my hopes up for a more simple, elegant Wonder creation process based on the one mentioned in the M20 core; But it seems, for better or for worse, that a good chunk of inspiration was drawn from Forged By Dragon's Fire - not necessarily a bad thing, given that a lot of people are used to it, but I still think an opportunity for streamlining it all was missed. That said, the section covers it all - all types of wonders, slight variations of the rules for technocratic devices, and a neat method of investing flaws and advantages into a wonder.<br /> The only thing that again made my eyelid twitch was that, once more, the example wonders had messed up types - Bullets specified as 'Gadgets' instead of 'Charms' and such. Oh well.</div> </div> </div> </div>The thing that bug me about the Wonders part is Charms and gadgets, the book says that they do no require an arete roll to work and even sleeper can use them, yet two line below it says that you need to roll an arete against difficulty 7, and some of the charms and gadget proposed don't event have an arete trait. I find it a bit confusing, but hey, maybye it's just me !","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12946-emerick","name":"Emerick","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12946_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"156"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-27T07:09:41-07:00","id":1121290,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Emerick</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1120963-book-of-secrets-on-dtrpg?p=1121285#post1121285\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The thing that bug me about the Wonders part is Charms and gadgets, the book says that they do no require an arete roll to work and even sleeper can use them, yet two line below it says that you need to roll an arete against difficulty 7, and some of the charms and gadget proposed don't event have an arete trait. I find it a bit confusing, but hey, maybye it's just me !</div> </div> </div> </div>Ah, be careful! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> Some wonders, including Charms/Gadgets and Talismans/Devices, have their *own* Arete dicepool, which gets used for that Arete roll. Other Wonders like Artifacts/Invetions depend on the Arete pool of the *user*. That's why the user does not need to be enlightened to use a charm - no Arete on the character sheet is required.<br /> <br /> And, as I said, the wonder example section is..flawed again, as it mixes up some types specified for the example wonders :|","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":6606,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","user":"Ambrosia","date":". 07-27-2017, 10:14 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-27T07:34:24-07:00","id":1121301,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ambrosia</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1120963-book-of-secrets-on-dtrpg?p=1121240#post1121240\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The chapter on <b>Justice and Influence</b> was an okay read to me. Admittedly, just like Cert\u00e1men, I am not highly interested in the unified formalities among the organized Council of the Nine Tradtions - mostly because I think those things really, really break down quickly as you close in on individual Tradition-, Chantry or Cabal politics and rules. I can see how they are all enforced in a place like the defunct Horizon realm of the same name, but on the 'front-lines' as the Technocracy would call it..things become much, much more individual where the Tradition's side of things is concerned.<br /> I mean, 'lazyness' and 'Cowardice' are defined as Low Crimes in the Tradition writeup. Really now. Those things *scream* of 'Thought up in council chambers, laughed at at the Cabal round-table'. Well, except if Hermetics are involved. Bwahah.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hm, really? This is a very light, so to say, view of the Traditions that you have.<br /> <br /> Who says - or, where is it written? - that the Verbenas or the Etherites can't follow and enforce rules to the same extent as the Hermetics?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3254-muad-dib","name":"Muad'Dib","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3254_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2059"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-27T07:55:34-07:00","id":1121308,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Muad'Dib</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1120963-book-of-secrets-on-dtrpg?p=1121301#post1121301\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Who says - or, where is it written? - that the Verbenas or the Etherites can't follow and enforce rules to the same extent as the Hermetics?</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, they can enforce rules. It's just that I believe that when it comes to the Council of Nine as a faction, that each Tradition, Chantry or Cabal is more bound to enforce its own variation of the rules than follow the official Partyline to the letter. Even more so when we break down things to the Chantry or Cabal level.<br /> <br /> Let's take 'cowardice' being defined as a crime on the Council level. It's good as a Partyline. Great as propaganda in view of the Ascension War.<br /> <br /> But can you see a traditional Verbena, which tend to be Mages highly connected to natural laws and primal emotions, define a natural urge and emotion like 'Cowardice' to be a *crime* to be punished?<br /> I think not.<br /> If somebody gets harmed because of somebody else's cowardice, I see them revaluating that person's abilities, perhaps lose confidence in them being able to be a good choice for certain missions - but not actually treating their natural reaction as a literal crime to be brought to tribunal. They are much more likely to try and *aid* that person with their problem of cowardice getting in the way, and try to strengthen their resolve in a helpful, natural manner, instead of punishing it. That or let Survival Of the Fittest handle it when you get to subfactions like the Lifeweavers. But a literal crime as the Partyline dictates? No.<br /> <br /> On the flipside, a Hermetic or likewise Tradition on the other hand would definitely see cowardice in face of a mission as a major failure on the Mage's part, and use disciplinary sanctioning accordingly, as a Mage's failure reflects on their House, if not the whole of the Order. Hermetics are about self-empowerment, control of creation, and discipline. Cowardice is no excuse. When House Quaesitor sends their representative, you better be ready.<br /> <br /> Likewise if we go down to Cabal level, if something goes wrong because of somebody's cowardice, I really do not see the Cabal reporting this up the chain to a point where it gets official sanctioned treatment as a Low Crime - Cabals tend to be actual friends. I am sure that in the vast majority of cases, the Cabal will handle it internally - and probably in a much more understanding manner than what the official rules dictate.<br /> <br /> This goes not for all things on the list of of course. Certain grisly things like murder, abuse, desertion, cooperating with the enemy, betrayal and such *definitely* make it up the grapevine and official levels much, much more universally, and independent of which group it happens in.<br /> <br /> --<br /> <br /> The Technocracy, on the other hand, is much more strict on all matters of breaking the Partyline no matter what level you go to - as are the Desparates out of necessity because of their high-risk infiltration gamble and secrecy. The much smaller margin for failure simply does not allow for much leeway and forgiveness.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> .","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":6606,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","user":"Ambrosia","date":". 07-27-2017, 11:22 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-27T13:59:42-07:00","id":1121518,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ambrosia</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1120963-book-of-secrets-on-dtrpg?p=1121240#post1121240\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Annnd that wraps up my review I guess.</div> </div> </div> </div>Very nice review!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1885-crackedman","name":"CrackedMan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1885_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"254"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-27T16:37:16-07:00","id":1121614,"title":"","content":"Haven't got the full book yet, but I'm looking at the table of contents, and it brings up a question or two.<br /> <br /> It shows Shapechanger Kin as being a Merit, and I expected that. But there's also a Merit called &quot;Natural Shapeshifter&quot;. Can anyone tell me what that is? Because I've been meaning to start a separate thread about something, and I want to know if Book of Secrets renders it moot.<br /> <br /> While we're on the subject of Merits and Flaws, I see we have both the returning Ghoul Merit, as well as a Flaw called &quot;Blood-Hungry Soul&quot;. I'd like to know what the latter is as well, if you all wouldn't mind. If it's what I think, it means M20 is rehashing that vitae-addicted Avatar business from Blood Treachery. And I don't think I need to tell you how well that's going to go down on this site.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-27T17:02:52-07:00","id":1121623,"title":"","content":"@Bluecho<br /> <br /> ShapeChanger Kin means your Kin folk to a were something, Natural Shapeshifter makes It easier to change your shape with life magic.<br /> <br /> Blood hungery soul means you in a past life you were a ghoul and now the soul calls out for the juice.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":13009,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13009-burritomage","user":"BurritoMage","date":". 07-27-2017, 08:16 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13009-burritomage","name":"BurritoMage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13009_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"585"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-27T17:31:47-07:00","id":1121631,"title":"","content":"Can you explain more about Natural Shapeshifter?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13055-goran","name":"Goran.","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"60"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-27T17:40:56-07:00","id":1121634,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Goran.</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1120963-book-of-secrets-on-dtrpg?p=1121631#post1121631\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Can you explain more about Natural Shapeshifter?</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200bYou still need to have the right life sphere levels but the difficulty to shapeshift as described in How did you do that is reduced by 2. It only effects you, not shape changing others.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13009-burritomage","name":"BurritoMage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13009_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"585"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-29T05:18:17-07:00","id":1122301,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Second Chances</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1120963-book-of-secrets-on-dtrpg#post1120963\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://&amp;quot;&#91;url\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/214133/M20-Book-of-Secrets?affiliate_id=498510&amp;quot;]I</a> think some of you have been waiting for this one...[/URL]<br /> <br /> Discuss.</div> </div> </div> </div>I am really not clear on the base level for a Grimoire. Page 160 says +1 per effect. The example of a primer given is 8 dots. Yet, the text of 143 implies grimoires can be lower than the rank of certain spheres. <br /> <br /> Forged by Dragons Fire broke it down like follows:<br /> Level of arete taught +1 per sphere.<br /> <br /> BoS does not follow this exactly. Thoughts?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2783-tytalus","name":"Tytalus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2783_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"491"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-30T02:10:08-07:00","id":1122652,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tytalus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1120963-book-of-secrets-on-dtrpg?p=1122301#post1122301\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I am really not clear on the base level for a Grimoire. Page 160 says +1 per effect. The example of a primer given is 8 dots. Yet, the text of 143 implies grimoires can be lower than the rank of certain spheres.<br /> <br /> Forged by Dragons Fire broke it down like follows:<br /> Level of arete taught +1 per sphere.<br /> <br /> BoS does not follow this exactly. Thoughts?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I like the system in BoS. Essentially you can have a Grimoire either grant cheaper purchase of Abilities, or Spheres, or Arete - or a mixture of each, with each of those effects (or multiple of the same) giving that +1 increase in Grimoire level, and requiring +3 more successes on the Arete roll on creation. I am not sure how page 143 implies that a Grimoire can be lower than the rank of spheres it teaches..it says quite the opposite in the Spheres section.<br /> <br /> <br /> Also, an important on page 151:<br /> &quot; a player does not have to spend experience and/or Background points to obtain a Wonder that the mage herself has created.&quot;<br /> <br /> As for the background cost of Wonders in general, it caused some confusion before:<br /> A wonder usually costs 1-3 XP/Freebies per background dots it is rated at, according to the table in the M20 corebook. Plus whatever XP/Freebie cost the Wonder's flaws or features add/subtract from that cost, as BoS page 157 shows.<br /> The example primer 'Oracle' has 4 background(!) dots - notice the small printed dots next to the name.<br /> It costs 8 XP or freebies to get. That's what the 'background cost X pts' next to each example is. It should have been labeled XP/Freebie cost.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":6606,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","user":"Ambrosia","date":". 07-30-2017, 05:13 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-31T18:46:49-07:00","id":1123264,"title":"","content":"Much as I expected, I found most of BoS of high quality and rather interesting and useful as the de facto second volume of the M20 corebook, especially the updated compilation of Merits and Flaws and the expanded foci chapter which got most of my attention and interest. The main issue I found with BoS is a continuation of the author's unfortunate tendency to use M20 books as a soapbox to preach about his pet peeves and twist setting and rules to fulfill them, which sometimes grows rather annoying for people with their own reasons to disagree, including yours truly.<br /> <br /> I'm generally very favorable to de-emphasized splats and freeform character development, so the way M20 corebook and BoS give ample leeway to custom-build all kinds of personal foci system from the bottom up, besides and beyond any group cultural stereotypes, found me enthusiastically agreeing. Although the M20 setting provides plenty of reasons for the Traditions, Technocracy, and Nephandi to keep existing as separate and hostile factions, I think this new system refreshingly blurs boundaries and de-emphasizes stuffy and rigid cultural stereotypes that separate groups within the same faction. A little experimentation at foci-building with the combination of M20 and BoS options showed me it is extremely easy to create plenty of characters that would fit fairly well in an handful different Traditions (or for that matter 2-3 Conventions) at once. This is a real character-customization godsend to me and I think should grow to be reflected in the Traditions structure to some serious degree; e.g. the Council should include a sizable number of quasi-Orphan 'hybrid' mages that do not easily fit in any given Tradition niche in ideological or membership terms, but rather span and settle the ground between multiple groups.<br /> <br /> As a rule, additional foci options in BoS look just as interesting and useful as the ones already provided in the corebook. However, the freeform foci rules make me seriously question the value of Dual Affiliation. Since you can easily create a personal foci system for any given character that gives free access to the better part of any two or three Traditions' practices and instruments, this Merit seems rather superfluous and outrageously overpriced. On second thoughts, however, I assume the residual value of Dual Affiliation may be strictly found at getting a second Affinity Sphere, and so the Merit effectively becomes an alternative variant of similarily-priced Sphere Natural (on the other hand, I wonder if that Merit is overpriced, too). Unfortunately BoS says nothing about how its new foci options typically align with established Traditions, which tends to reduce their usefulness, esp. when they are exclusive or dominant in a character concept.<br /> <br /> As it concerns the Merits and Flaws section, I find it another veritable treasuretrove of character development ideas that works very well to customize the character concept even much beyond what the other Traits and the foci rules can do. The options are so abundant and interesting I'm favorable to seriously loose limits about the number of Merits and Flaws a character may get at creation, and even more so be permissive about buying them during play with any barely decent story justification.<br /> <br /> I only found a few issues that leave me puzzled: e.g. the Beast Within Flaw makes a lot of sense conceptwise, but the rule about checking for frenzy is nonsensical. The RAW makes it so it gets more difficult for a mage to resist frenzy with an higher Willpower. Becoming less able to control one's rage the more more willful you get makes no sense and is nothing like the way all other WoD characters with impulse-control issues make such checks. So I assume the rule needs to be changed so that the player rolls say Avatar vs. a 10-half Willpower difficulty. Or alternatively rolling Willpower vs. Avatar x2 difficulty.<br /> <br /> Moreover, the Enchanting/Repulsive Merit/Flaw seems to assume any character is going to have only one such special feature. But one can easily think of several cases when it makes sense for a character to have multiple features, e.g. a mage that has an enchanting gaze or a seductive or commanding voice, or for that matter having an 'evil eye' and being very graceful. So I assume it should be possible to buy multiple versions of the M/F, or for that matter combine the Merit and the Flaw, as long as they concern different physical features.<br /> <br /> I'm usually little interested in playing characters heavily invested in magical items, so I paid scarce attention so far at the Wonders chapter, and I think this game has too many Secondary Traits already. The Resonance and Synergy rules look very interesting as a concept but somewhat confusing in application, so I need to further ponder and experiment on them. The Justice and Influence chapter was a very mixed issue for me, sometimes looking interesting: e.g. the way Branding became kind of non-lethal standard sentence for serious crimes in the Traditions, although I wonder what keeps powerful mages from using their Spheres to remove a Brand before end of sentence.<br /> <br /> Sometimes it made me shake my head: e.g. if you ask my opinion, the author went too far out of PC political bias in making Indenture a taboo in several cases, since mage society doesn't have many other feasible options to make Awakened offenders do restitution (and it makes no sense for the Choristers to shun it). The 'SJW' Disparates and their draconian 'justice' system that makes routine use of mutilation and torture, dear Universe. The Technocracy are totalitarian monsters, but how the heck this bunch of zealots is supposed to be a better alternative? The Certamen system is a welcome comeback and seems OK to me.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1949,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","user":"Irioth","date":". 08-01-2017, 09:06 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","name":"Irioth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"566"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-31T22:22:25-07:00","id":1123316,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Irioth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1120963-book-of-secrets-on-dtrpg?p=1123264#post1123264\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I only found a few issues that leave me puzzled: e.g. the Beast Within Flaw makes a lot of sense conceptwise, but the rule about checking for frenzy is nonsensical. The RAW makes it so it gets more difficult for a mage to resist frenzy with an higher Willpower. Becoming less able to control one's rage the more more willful you get makes no sense and is nothing like the way all other WoD characters with impulse-control issues make such checks. So I assume the rule needs to be changed so that the player rolls say Avatar vs. a 10-half Willpower difficulty. Or alternatively rolling Willpower vs. Avatar x2 difficulty.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, this confused me at first as well - however, the quote that got me thinking, as written, is this:<br /> <br /> &quot;Considering that the Avatar could be seen as your inner<br /> Beast, a character with the Pattern Essence cannot take this<br /> Flaw. It\u2019s best suited for Dynamic Essences, although a Quest-<br /> ing or Primordial Avatar could attain frightening rages too.&quot;<br /> <br /> Now add to that the fact that Beast Within is a supernatural(!) flaw as opposed to a mental one, plus the fact that in this particular berserk state the Mage is very able and downright urged to use True Magick instead of mindless physical violence..well. It makes it clear that the beast going on a rampage is not a human mind, but the Avatar itself. And the reason for the Willpower being the difficulty becomes more clear;<br /> The Avatar's symbiosis with the Mage (regardless of speculations of what the Avatar actualy *is*) and their True Magick gets fueled by sheer will. Willpower. The more potent in will the Mage is, the stronger the connection to their Avatar, the more easily the Avatar can influence the Mage (or vice versa). It is a little ironic and illogical sounding at first, given how we usually associate strong will with the ability to *resist* external or sudden changes...but the Avatar is anything but external.<br /> After all, it is the most strong willed Mages that become the most devoted to the paradigm and Magick their Avatar drives them towards.<br /> <br /> Now, the Avatar's *own* willpower could be seen as its Avatar rating - the stronger the avatar in essence, the more in control of itself it is. And thus to resist the frenzy, the amount of dice you get is that rating (+1), or the resonance, whichever is higher.<br /> <br /> I think the whole thing makes for a particularly interesting (if tough) flaw.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":6606,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","user":"Ambrosia","date":". 08-01-2017, 01:32 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-01T17:33:33-07:00","id":1123718,"title":"","content":"I see your argument and the gist of it sure makes sense conceptwise (make no mistake this Flaw's fluff works nicely and the Flaw is excellent for certain concepts) but the RAW still makes no sense and your argument fails to justify it. Sure, in this case the Avatar is what fuels the mage's rage and it is an integral, 'internal' part of the mage. But we have several examples in the WoD of supernatural splats that face very similar circumstances and in no case their triggers for frenzy work remotely like this. And it outlandish to argue that a vampire's Beast is any less integral and 'internal' to the character than a mage's Avatar. In all other cases, a character's Willpower is more or less what they use to resist their violent urges, not what empowers them. Sure, if the Avatar is what fuels the mage's rage, it makes sense to roll its score to check for frenzy. And the mage's Awakened Will empowers their feats of True Magick. But barring extreme special cases (e.g. Marauders) the Will of the mage is always channeled and controlled by the character's consciousness, not the Avatar.<br /> <br /> So it makes no sense if the raging Avatar rolls against Willpower to unleash a frenzy, and it occurs if the roll <u>fails</u>. At the very least it should be the Avatar rolls against Willpower (adding a +1 or substituting it with Resonance if higher makes no particular problem) and frenzy occurs if the roll <u>succeeds</u>. The alternative would make exactly as much (non)sense as vampires or werewolves being more prone to frenzy the more willful and self-driven they are. Again, with this Flaw the Avatar essentally gets to play the role of a vampire's Beast, so it only makes sense they would behave in a very similar way. Invert the outcome of the roll, and the rule can make sense for the fluff; the Beast-like Avatar wants to rage when presented with the right stimulus, but the mage's self can use their own Will to try and keep it under control. The more powerful the Avatar, or the less developed the mage's Will, the more difficult stopping frenzy. The less strong the Avatar, or the more willful the mage, the easier is to suppress frenzy. This way, Willpower can be used as the main tool for the mage to remain in control of themselves, just like it happens for all other WoD characters that face an impulse control problem, regardless of whether it is fuelled by psychological or supernatural circumstances. Willpower acting against the mage's wish is contradiction and nonsense. Of course the case may occur when the mage wants frenzy to occur, just like a vampire riding the wave, esp. because a character may use magick during it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1949,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","user":"Irioth","date":". 08-01-2017, 10:49 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","name":"Irioth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"566"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-02T02:59:11-07:00","id":1123811,"title":"","content":"Man, now I wish the errata period wasn't over - now I want a clarification on that flaw's mechanics :|<br /> <br /> Yeah, what you say makes sense for consistency's sake. And I *do* wonder if it was supposed to say 'if the roll succeeds'. Hrm.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-02T05:44:26-07:00","id":1123844,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ambrosia</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1120963-book-of-secrets-on-dtrpg?p=1123811#post1123811\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Man, now I wish the errata period wasn't over - now I want a clarification on that flaw's mechanics :|<br /> <br /> Yeah, what you say makes sense for consistency's sake. And I *do* wonder if it was supposed to say 'if the roll succeeds'. Hrm.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yep, I kick myself for having failed to notice this issue before the errata period was over. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/redface.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Embarrassment\" smilieid=\"2\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> I assume the wording in all likelihood was supposed to say that. At least between the two of us we puzzled out its likely meaning.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1949,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","user":"Irioth","date":". 08-02-2017, 10:18 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","name":"Irioth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"566"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-24T03:14:25-07:00","id":1133312,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Fat Larry</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1120963-book-of-secrets-on-dtrpg?p=1121076#post1121076\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Yeah.<br /> <br /> While I'm enjoying most of what I've read so far, I'm getting a bit too much of Phil's own personal life stories and personal opinions here.<br /> <br /> Page count definitely could've been used in more useful ways, IMO.</div> </div> </div> </div>Really? It's pretty uncontroversial stuff. He uses the FAQ to respond to common questions he faces, and he responds to them in pretty moderate ways which I feel are at the core of Mage.<br /> <br /> I think most of the concern comes from his response to &quot;Why is Mage so liberal&quot;, but he adequately answers this by pointing to negative traits in every Tradition, Craft etc, in-universe conservatives, and conservative ideas at the very core of Mage (many paradigms hark back to a Pre-Lapsarian concept where everything USED to be perfect, which is about as conservative as you can get really), whilst also justifying the liberal concepts (the basic idea of the game is changing reality to become better than it is right now, in your own opinion, which is going to lead to a lot of progressive elements). &quot;Everything should be the way it is right now, basically&quot; isn't a Paradigm. Paradigms aren't stagnant things, they are moving and evolving, which leaves *more* room for progressive ideology than conservative ideology, short of the aforementioned &quot;everything used to be good, and we need to go back to that&quot; paradigms, which are only really &quot;conservative&quot; in a broad sense (obviously that's a word which has multiple meanings and means different things to different people).<br /> <br /> I simply enjoyed reading it because he clearly understands that written work is heavily tied to the person who writes it, and he doesn't apologize for being a person with opinions and beliefs which not every reader will agree with. Perhaps I only see it as reasoned and relatively neutral because it agrees with my own beliefs, but I would like to point out that &quot;hey, maybe colonialism was a bad thing and some Mages would be pissed about its aftereffects and the destruction of their cultures&quot; is about as uncontroversial a &quot;progressive&quot; statement as a person could possibly make, and practically every so-called liberal element falls into a camp of &quot;hey, obviously bad things are bad, and obviously good things are good&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 08-24-2017, 06:26 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-24T07:58:03-07:00","id":1133372,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"> &quot;hey, maybe colonialism was a bad thing and some Mages would be pissed about its aftereffects and the destruction of their cultures&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, the Traditions were supposed to protect mystical practices, and those are tied to culture. So one would think that this would be among their agenda.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, it wasn't Hermetic, Verbena, nor Chorister (christian branch) culture what was decimated, an thus these Traditions weren't crying blood tears at the whole issue, even when they should be (taking in consideration the supposed &quot;purpose&quot; of the Council). This is why the Dreamspeakers are disapointed with the Trads, they dind't join to see choristers build churches in their ancestral lands.<br /> <br /> What I don't like about M20 progresivism it's Disparates being progre. Most Crafts were composed of the mystics that refused to join the Council because this would compromise their beliefs. They prefered death to having to join another Paradigm. They remained isolated and true to their traditions (no mater how conservative they were) trough all of the atrocities of the &quot;age of exploration/conquest&quot;, they refused changing their ways in spite of colonization. They faced Comunism and Capitalism ushered by the Technocracy...And now, in the face of 21 century, they are going to back down from their ancestral ways?<br /> I don't like Templars allowing women to enter their order. I don't like Taftani accepting the modern world and becoming rich industrialists. For these crafts, becoming progresive seems like a claudication.<br /> <br /> I preffer to make Disparate Aliance a loose net of truly <i>Traditional </i>Traditions. While the Council of Nine becomes more and more new age, accepting technomancers among the members of all Traditions, while coonservative elements loose power (Sons of Ether becoming Society of Ether) and all that, the Aliance would be the conservative that trully follow <i>Tradition</i>. They infiltrate into the Traditions and accept help from other Crafts only because they're facing greater enemies, but they know that, in the end, they're all enemy.<br /> The exeptions would be the Holowers, that are here because they betrayed the Council of Nine (and thus both the Technocracy and the Traditions hate them), and the Batini, who are the masterminds of the Aliance, providing the subtle connection networks. Batini do this in an effort to reach Unity. They hope to replace the Traditions with their own, better, Council, but of course this ain't going to be easy because Disparate are notoriously stubborn.<br /> But that's just my headcanon.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 08-24-2017, 11:01 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}